   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Lectures 11–13.** Expected values, variances, and the properties of expectation and variance.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "eaf319c5-b947-4be8-b90d-07e4ea4af2c2",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "c94dcea8-d0e5-417e-9460-f7920a68553e"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "6039489c-426b-46ac-8fd2-e52106e4a341"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "52e9fc11-81ed-4bab-a495-7e4b29f79a8b",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "72ec1f08-5a0f-478f-8963-c20f6396f281"
    }
   ],
   "id": "c7266b54-3f93-5296-bc72-5ec6d5e36be2"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 1,
   "text": "Topic 1: Expected value",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "196eedf3-d948-5933-981b-93e19317f10a"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "The **expected value** (mean) $E[X]=\\mu_X$ is the **long-run average** of $X$ if you repeat the experiment many times.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "332f3fdb-d003-5bf4-8a83-111cecf07ca1"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Each value is weighted by how likely it is: a **probability-weighted average**.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "b14f98a7-69f3-5333-9ef3-e8a17ac00994"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "E[X]=\\sum_{x\\in R_X} x\\,p_X(x)\\quad\\text{(discrete)}",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "eaf319c5-b947-4be8-b90d-07e4ea4af2c2",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "2cc1ee41-e6c3-4fd5-a41c-67fb0c8d5b40"
    }
   ],
   "id": "c987a08e-75fe-5741-81cd-6a0281c27d11"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "E[X]=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x\\,f_X(x)\\,dx\\quad\\text{(continuous)}",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "eaf319c5-b947-4be8-b90d-07e4ea4af2c2",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "2cc1ee41-e6c3-4fd5-a41c-67fb0c8d5b40"
    }
   ],
   "id": "8446d2ac-d841-5f16-bfde-729bdaa907c9"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "**Why it works:** repeat $N$ times; value $x_k$ shows up about $N p_k$ times, so the average is $\\approx\\sum x_k p_k$.",
    "**Continuous:** the sum becomes an integral, with $p_k$ replaced by $f_X(x)\\,dx$.",
    "$E[X]$ **need not be a possible value**: a die averages $3.5$.",
    "**Mixed** $X$ (a point mass plus a density): add the two parts, $\\sum x\\,P(X=x)+\\int x\\,f(x)\\,dx$."
   ],
   "ordered": false,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "eaf319c5-b947-4be8-b90d-07e4ea4af2c2",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "c94dcea8-d0e5-417e-9460-f7920a68553e"
    }
   ],
   "id": "2a694c55-ccfb-5e55-89ff-c92f7849636a"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Expected value of a function** $g(X)$: no need to find the distribution of $Y=g(X)$ first. Just weight $g(x)$ instead of $x$:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "91d1e033-f294-5ffd-adf5-8464fed5014e"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "E[g(X)]=\\sum_{x} g(x)\\,p_X(x)\\qquad E[g(X)]=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} g(x)\\,f_X(x)\\,dx",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "87da869b-2cf9-4dd0-8397-b436da482e58"
    }
   ],
   "id": "8fd39069-5459-58e0-bf61-d0827c8f8a7a"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "tone": "warning",
   "text": "**$E[X^2]\\ne(E[X])^2$** in general. Square inside the sum/integral, not after.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "87da869b-2cf9-4dd0-8397-b436da482e58"
    }
   ],
   "id": "7b1be2a3-c8ca-5a77-b173-19d3a360232f"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "tone": "reminder",
   "text": "**Calculus you need for $E[X]$**\n\n- **Power rule:** $\\displaystyle\\int x^n\\,dx=\\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}$.\n- **Exponential:** $\\displaystyle\\int e^{-\\lambda x}\\,dx=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda}e^{-\\lambda x}$.\n- **By parts:** $\\displaystyle\\int u\\,dv=uv-\\int v\\,du$. For $\\int x\\,e^{-\\lambda x}dx$ take $u=x$, $dv=e^{-\\lambda x}dx$.\n- **Shortcut (memorise):** $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\infty}x^n e^{-\\lambda x}\\,dx=\\frac{n!}{\\lambda^{n+1}}$.\n- **Symmetry:** an **odd** function ($g(-x)=-g(x)$, like $x$, $x^3$) on $[-a,a]$ integrates to $0$.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "73f0f1f7-3757-57c0-bf66-a67ae6fed10e"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Method:**",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "3cca6006-5fe2-51a1-99eb-c9e231d974a3"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "**Write** the PMF/PDF and where it is nonzero.",
    "**Multiply** by $x$ (or by $g(x)$).",
    "**Add/integrate** only over the nonzero range (outside it the integrand is $0$)."
   ],
   "ordered": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "2abeb093-dc51-5d71-b4f2-aae7b09298a8"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 1 · A die and a uniform",
   "prompt": "(a) A fair die is rolled; $X$ = the number. Find $E[X]$ and $E[X^2]$. (b) $X$ is uniform on $[0,2]$: $f_X(x)=\\tfrac12$ on $[0,2]$. Find $E[X]$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "(a) **Weight** each face by $\\tfrac16$.",
     "latex": "E[X]=\\frac16(1+2+3+4+5+6)=\\frac{21}{6}=3.5"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "$E[X^2]$: square **inside** the sum.",
     "latex": "E[X^2]=\\frac16(1+4+9+16+25+36)=\\frac{91}{6}\\approx15.17"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "Compare: $(E[X])^2=12.25\\ne15.17$.",
     "latex": "E[X^2]\\ne(E[X])^2"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "(b) **Integrate** over $[0,2]$ only.",
     "latex": "E[X]=\\int_0^2 x\\cdot\\frac12\\,dx=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right]_0^2=1"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "(a) $E[X]=3.5$, $E[X^2]=\\tfrac{91}{6}$ (b) $E[X]=1$, the middle of $[0,2]$.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "eaf319c5-b947-4be8-b90d-07e4ea4af2c2",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "81443470-b993-498d-9945-558851b03e47"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "87da869b-2cf9-4dd0-8397-b436da482e58"
    }
   ],
   "id": "cb04df4c-c597-5dc5-98da-4950f2cc1793"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "The mean is the **balance point** of the PDF. A long right tail pulls it to the right of the peak.\n\n```graph\ntitle \"Exponential PDF: the mean $1/\\lambda$ is not the peak\"\nx 0 6 label=\"$x$\" height=240\ny 0 2.1 label=\"$f_X(x)$\"\nslider L 0.3 2 1 step=0.05 label=\"$\\lambda$\"\ncurve \"L*exp(-L*x)\" from=0 to=6 label=\"$\\lambda e^{-\\lambda x}$\"\nvline \"1/L\" label=\"$E[X]=1/\\lambda$\" color=2\nreadout \"$E[X]=1/\\lambda$\" \"1/L\"\n```",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "a26eb506-2e1e-5fcb-8ea4-8baa81c355c8"
  },
  {
   "type": "practice_problem",
   "title": "Try it 1 · Second moment of a uniform",
   "difficulty": "introductory",
   "origin": "source",
   "prompt": "$X$ is uniform on $[0,2]$. Find $E[X^2]$.",
   "solution": "",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Use** $E[g(X)]=\\int g(x)f_X(x)\\,dx$ with $g(x)=x^2$.",
     "latex": "E[X^2]=\\int_0^2 x^2\\cdot\\frac12\\,dx"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Power rule.**",
     "latex": "=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^2=\\frac12\\cdot\\frac83=\\frac43"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$E[X^2]=\\tfrac43$ (not $(E[X])^2=1$).",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "1fa5f1c7-2d1d-4842-a1f9-1949b30bc7b2"
    }
   ],
   "id": "9522fc3a-43ae-505c-896f-c57d6e5beddd"
  },
  {
   "type": "practice_problem",
   "title": "Try it 2 · Mean time between customers",
   "difficulty": "intermediate",
   "origin": "source",
   "prompt": "The time $X$ between customer arrivals is exponential: $f_X(t)=\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}$ for $t\\ge0$. Find the mean $E[X]$.",
   "solution": "",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Set up** over $[0,\\infty)$.",
     "latex": "E[X]=\\int_0^{\\infty}t\\,\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}\\,dt"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**By parts:** $u=t$, $dv=\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}dt$, so $du=dt$, $v=-e^{-\\lambda t}$.",
     "latex": "=\\Big[-t\\,e^{-\\lambda t}\\Big]_0^{\\infty}+\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-\\lambda t}\\,dt"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "The boundary term is $0$ (exponential beats $t$).",
     "latex": "=0+\\Big[-\\frac1\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}\\Big]_0^{\\infty}=\\frac1\\lambda"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$E[X]=\\dfrac1\\lambda$. (Rate 3 per minute → mean gap $\\tfrac13$ min.)",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "f017b9e2-c705-4ab7-a4d1-e289ed6c90bf",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "5d0c04fd-0343-4ac4-b647-98627bcad82c"
    }
   ],
   "id": "bbc1462a-91e1-5f94-9e3c-1fd4ab053f8d"
  },
  {
   "type": "practice_problem",
   "title": "Try it 3 · Mean of a symmetric PDF",
   "difficulty": "intermediate",
   "origin": "source",
   "prompt": "$X$ has PDF $f_X(t)=\\tfrac{\\lambda}{2}e^{-\\lambda|t-\\gamma|}$ for all $t$ (a Laplace density centred at $\\gamma$). Find $E[X]$.",
   "solution": "",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Shift:** let $u=t-\\gamma$, so $t=u+\\gamma$.",
     "latex": "E[X]=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}(u+\\gamma)\\,\\frac{\\lambda}{2}e^{-\\lambda|u|}\\,du"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Split.** $u\\,e^{-\\lambda|u|}$ is **odd**, so its integral is $0$; the rest is $\\gamma$ times the total area $1$.",
     "latex": "=\\underbrace{\\int u\\,\\frac{\\lambda}{2}e^{-\\lambda|u|}du}_{0}+\\gamma\\underbrace{\\int\\frac{\\lambda}{2}e^{-\\lambda|u|}du}_{1}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "So the mean is the centre of symmetry.",
     "latex": "E[X]=\\gamma"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$E[X]=\\gamma$: a PDF symmetric about $c$ has mean $c$.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "f017b9e2-c705-4ab7-a4d1-e289ed6c90bf",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "5d0c04fd-0343-4ac4-b647-98627bcad82c"
    }
   ],
   "id": "db82c0c0-257f-5ea7-932b-bb048e5b054c"
  },
  {
   "type": "practice_problem",
   "title": "Try it 4 · Mean of a mixed random variable",
   "difficulty": "intermediate",
   "origin": "source",
   "prompt": "A received signal level $X$ is $0$ with probability $0.2$ (signal lost). Otherwise (probability $0.8$) it is uniform on $(0,10)$, so the continuous part has density $0.8\\cdot\\tfrac1{10}=0.08$ on $(0,10)$. Find $E[X]$.",
   "solution": "",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Discrete part:** the point mass at $0$.",
     "latex": "0\\cdot P(X=0)=0\\cdot0.2=0"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Continuous part:** integrate $x$ times the density $0.08$.",
     "latex": "\\int_0^{10}x\\,(0.08)\\,dx=0.08\\cdot\\frac{10^2}{2}=4"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Add** the parts.",
     "latex": "E[X]=0+4=4"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$E[X]=4$ (the 20% of zeros pull the average down from 5).",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "acf5b4b4-e7d3-4cc1-a3d0-ab8651d3dc89",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "2765690d-ffea-4bba-8998-f9b1095c0dc0"
    }
   ],
   "id": "a9bff649-6281-5791-9a86-57efabd27e94"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 1,
   "text": "Topic 2: Variance and standard deviation",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "ad90db04-cda1-51f4-9f44-ea0beec62a9a"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "The mean gives the **centre**. Two variables can share a centre but spread very differently:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "8142bb62-d818-5105-943c-0c12f0ca679d"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "$X=9$ or $11$ (each $\\tfrac12$): $E[X]=10$, stays close.",
    "$Y=0$ or $20$ (each $\\tfrac12$): $E[Y]=10$, far more spread out.",
    "The average deviation $E[X-\\mu_X]$ is always $0$ (the $+$ and $-$ cancel), so we **square** the deviation."
   ],
   "ordered": false,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "0eb0a16e-6d2c-40a6-83e1-031a1f0dae55"
    }
   ],
   "id": "be840fd8-7381-5fa7-bc13-7113bfce954f"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E\\big[(X-\\mu_X)^2\\big]",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "6039489c-426b-46ac-8fd2-e52106e4a341"
    }
   ],
   "id": "9ceaa3fd-216f-51fa-a409-e8a6f75eb96f"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Shortcut** (almost always faster):",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "6c1b0724-bdd9-5b1a-9e26-e3c5f7778c27"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-\\big(E[X]\\big)^2",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "6039489c-426b-46ac-8fd2-e52106e4a341"
    }
   ],
   "id": "47b81ed9-3a58-5163-8f74-57215aee0fde"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "```steps\ntitle \"Where the shortcut comes from\"\n$$\nE\\big[(X-\\mu)^2\\big]\n$$\nStart from the definition, with $\\mu=E[X]$ a constant.\n$$\nE\\big[\\hl{X^2-2\\mu X+\\mu^2}\\big]\n$$\nExpand the square.\n$$\n\\hl{E[X^2]-2\\mu\\,E[X]+\\mu^2}\n$$\nLinearity: constants come out of $E$.\n$$\nE[X^2]-2\\mu\\cdot\\hl{\\mu}+\\mu^2\n$$\nUse $E[X]=\\mu$.\n$$\n\\hl{E[X^2]-\\mu^2}\n$$\nDone: mean of the square minus square of the mean.\n```",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "979b3da0-9d6a-5142-b54e-fcb3c8fe831b"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "**Units:** variance has **squared** units (V → V²).",
    "**Standard deviation** $\\sigma_X=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}$ is back in the units of $X$: a typical distance from the mean.",
    "$\\operatorname{Var}(X)\\ge0$, and $=0$ only when $X$ is a constant.",
    "For a Gaussian $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$: mean $\\mu$, standard deviation $\\sigma$ (the width of the bell)."
   ],
   "ordered": false,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "27f2e489-3e85-4171-b6d6-358170b19bc4"
    }
   ],
   "id": "18b23beb-8135-581b-9f88-84b74532b9fa"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Drag $d$: the mean never moves, but the variance grows like $d^2$. ($d=1$ is $X$ above, $d=10$ is $Y$.)\n\n```graph\ntitle \"Same mean, different spread: $X=10\\pm d$, each with probability $\\tfrac12$\"\nx -1 21 label=\"$x$\" height=220\ny 0 0.65 label=\"$p_X(x)$\"\nslider d 0 10 1 step=0.5 label=\"$d$\"\nsegment \"10-d\" \"0\" \"10-d\" \"0.5\" color=1\npoint \"10-d\" \"0.5\" color=1\nsegment \"10+d\" \"0\" \"10+d\" \"0.5\" color=1\npoint \"10+d\" \"0.5\" color=1\nvline \"10\" label=\"$E[X]=10$\" color=2\nreadout \"$E[X]$\" \"10\"\nreadout \"$\\mathrm{Var}(X)=d^2$\" \"d^2\"\nreadout \"$\\sigma_X=d$\" \"d\"\n```",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "1160e827-4206-5345-a1fb-7fa20774b60f"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Method:**",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "d582323e-4acd-573a-9338-c045384c788a"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "**Find** $E[X]$.",
    "**Find** $E[X^2]$ (square inside the sum/integral).",
    "**Subtract:** $\\operatorname{Var}=E[X^2]-(E[X])^2$; then $\\sigma=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}}$."
   ],
   "ordered": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "b322d238-f7ad-5512-afa6-6b618bedb839"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 2 · A two-level sensor voltage",
   "prompt": "A sensor reads $X=4\\text{ V}$ or $X=6\\text{ V}$, each with probability $0.5$. Find $E[X]$, $E[X^2]$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)$ and $\\sigma_X$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Mean.**",
     "latex": "E[X]=4(0.5)+6(0.5)=5\\text{ V}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Second moment.**",
     "latex": "E[X^2]=16(0.5)+36(0.5)=26\\text{ V}^2"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Subtract.**",
     "latex": "\\operatorname{Var}(X)=26-5^2=1\\text{ V}^2"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Standard deviation** (back to volts).",
     "latex": "\\sigma_X=\\sqrt1=1\\text{ V}"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$E[X]=5$ V, $E[X^2]=26$ V², $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1$ V², $\\sigma_X=1$ V.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "b8fcca89-f00b-4b0a-8a73-1aec471f4407",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "7e0019b1-d1d3-4c15-8850-3e91dc4e8719"
    }
   ],
   "id": "9cc8283d-7489-54f4-8b05-a2cad96ff483"
  },
  {
   "type": "practice_problem",
   "title": "Try it 5 · A binary random variable",
   "difficulty": "introductory",
   "origin": "source",
   "prompt": "$X$ takes only the values $0$ and $1$, with $P(X=0)=p$ and $P(X=1)=1-p$. Find $E[X]$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)$ and $E[X^5]$.",
   "solution": "",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Mean.**",
     "latex": "E[X]=0\\cdot p+1\\cdot(1-p)=1-p"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Trick:** $0^n=0$ and $1^n=1$, so $X^n=X$ for every $n\\ge1$.",
     "latex": "E[X^2]=E[X^5]=E[X]=1-p"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Subtract.**",
     "latex": "\\operatorname{Var}(X)=(1-p)-(1-p)^2=(1-p)\\,p"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$E[X]=1-p$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=p(1-p)$, $E[X^5]=1-p$.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "a2bcf96c-29bc-4570-bbde-f69160346624",
     "version": 1,
