   "difficulty": "advanced",
   "origin": "source",
   "prompt": "For a **nonnegative** continuous $X$ with CDF $F_X$, show that $E[X]=\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\infty}\\big(1-F_X(t)\\big)\\,dt$ (assume $t\\,(1-F_X(t))\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty$). Then use it to find the mean of an exponential.",
   "solution": "",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**By parts** with $u=1-F_X(t)$ and $dv=dt$: then $du=-f_X(t)\\,dt$ and $v=t$.",
     "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}(1-F_X(t))\\,dt=\\Big[t\\,(1-F_X(t))\\Big]_0^{\\infty}+\\int_0^{\\infty}t\\,f_X(t)\\,dt"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "The boundary term is $0$ at both ends (given at $\\infty$; $t=0$ at the bottom).",
     "latex": "=0+E[X]"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Exponential:** $1-F_X(t)=e^{-\\lambda t}$.",
     "latex": "E[X]=\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-\\lambda t}\\,dt=\\frac1\\lambda"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "Shown; for the exponential $E[X]=\\tfrac1\\lambda$ with no by-parts on $x e^{-\\lambda x}$.",
